label: Verdict
description: Joins both checks for one revision — the fan-in this example exists to demonstrate
input:
  allOf:
    - StyleReport
    - FactReport
output: ReviewNote
examples:
  - given:
      StyleReport:
        revision_id: "m-1-r1"
        note: "style ok at round 1"
      FactReport:
        revision_id: "m-1-r1"
        note: "facts ok at round 1"
    expect:
      ReviewNote:
        revision_id: "m-1-r1"
        combined: "style ok at round 1 | facts ok at round 1"
properties:
  # The invariant the whole example exists to demonstrate, stated in the
  # contract rather than only asserted in a test: a note is about the
  # revision its reports were about. If the lineage join ever paired reports
  # from different rounds, this is what would catch it at the acceptance
  # gate rather than in a run.
  - name: note names the revision its reports were about
    description: >-
      Against BOTH reports, deliberately. Comparing the output to only the
      style report was a false green: an implementation handed reports from
      different rounds could project one of them and pass, which is the
      cross-revision pairing this example exists to rule out. Conjoined, a
      mismatched bag admits no valid output, so the contract forces the
      implementation to reject it.
    expr:
      and:
        - eq:
            - get: output.revision_id
            - get: input.StyleReport.revision_id
        - eq:
            - get: output.revision_id
            - get: input.FactReport.revision_id
